SystemVerilog types and GTKWave extensions (test/data/README.md).

Verilator's own VCD writer, IEEE 1364 types only:

  $ ./vcd_dump data/verilator/sv_types.vcd
  file:      sv_types.vcd
  date:      (none)
  version:   Generated by VerilatedVcd
  timescale: 1ps
  scope module TOP
    var wire 1 clk
    scope module sv_types
      var wire 1 clk
      var wire 1 b
      var wire 8 by [7:0]
      var wire 16 si [15:0]
      var wire 32 i [31:0]
      var wire 64 li [63:0]
      var wire 8 lg [7:0]
      var wire 32 cnt [31:0]
      var wire 2 st [1:0]
      var wire 12 pkt [11:0]
      var real 64 r
  events:    290 (timestamps: 40, changes: 250, dumps: 0, comments: 0)

The same simulation traced as FST and converted by fst2vcd -e: SystemVerilog
$var types, and enum attributes inside scopes:

  $ ./vcd_dump data/verilator/sv_types.fst2vcd.vcd
  file:      sv_types.fst2vcd.vcd
  date:      Thu Oct 15 03:31:19 2026
  version:   fstWriter
  timescale: 1ps
  scope module TOP
    var wire 1 clk
    scope module sv_types
      var wire 1 clk
      var bit 1 b
      var byte 8 by [7:0]
      var shortint 16 si [15:0]
      var int 32 i [31:0]
      var longint 64 li [63:0]
      var logic 8 lg [7:0]
      var integer 32 cnt [31:0]
      var logic 2 st [1:0]
      var logic 12 pkt [11:0]
      var real 64 r
  events:    291 (timestamps: 40, changes: 250, dumps: 1, comments: 0)

Both traces carry the same value changes:

  $ ./vcd_dump --summary data/verilator/sv_types.vcd | tail -1
  events:    290 (timestamps: 40, changes: 250, dumps: 0, comments: 0)
  $ ./vcd_dump --summary data/verilator/sv_types.fst2vcd.vcd | tail -1
  events:    291 (timestamps: 40, changes: 250, dumps: 1, comments: 0)

Everything libfst writes: 9-state scalars and vectors, strings (including
empty ones), port values, SystemVerilog and VHDL scope types, comments and
attributes:

  $ ./vcd_dump data/fst2vcd/kitchen.vcd
  file:      kitchen.vcd
  date:      Wed Oct 14 12:00:00 2026
  version:   fst_ref a2
  timescale: 1ns
  scope module top
    var wire 1 clk
    var reg 1 states
    var logic 8 b8[7:0]
    var reg 13 b13
    var bit 130 wide
    var real 64 r
    var shortreal 32 sr
    var realtime 64 rt
    var integer 32 i
    var parameter 4 P
    var event 1 ev
    var string 0 msg
    var port 4 p
    var wire 8 b8_alias
    var real 64 r_alias
    var wire 4 d1
    var wire 4 d2
    var wire 1 d3
    var wire 1 n1
    var wire 3 never
    scope generate gen[0]
      var int 32 gv
    scope begin $unnamed_scope_0
      var wire 1 unnamed_clk
    scope begin $unnamed_scope_1
    scope vhdl_architecture arch
      var bit 1 ast
    scope sv_array sva
  events:    2102 (timestamps: 401, changes: 1698, dumps: 3, comments: 0)

The initial values, resolved: 9-state, port, string (empty included) and real
values:

  $ ./vcd_dump --resolve --range 0-0 data/fst2vcd/kitchen.vcd | sed -n '/^#0/,$p'
  #0
  $dumpvars
    top.d2=hb
    top.d1=hb
    top.p=puzu0uu1xzxZ00U
    top.msg=s
    top.rt=0.
    top.sr=0.
    top.r_alias=0.
    top.r=0.
    top.wide=bu-0zxlxzux1z--00huhxuwhluzlxhzh-hhww1huzlzwhxhl0h00wwxxlz0-1l1xhz-z-ux1lxwxwzl000-w0u-zz-uzlx0-zlxx0zhhh01luuxu-lxxxlhzzhlwhhxx-1l
    top.b13=h140b
    top.b8_alias=bx10xxzxx
    top.b8[7:0]=bx10xxzxx
    top.states=0
    top.arch.ast=0
    top.$unnamed_scope_0.unnamed_clk=0
    top.clk=0
  $end

An unexpected character in the value changes is an error with its position,
not skipped:

  $ printf '$scope module m $end\n$var wire 1 ! a $end\n$upscope $end\n$enddefinitions $end\n#0\n1!\n%%oops\n' > bad.vcd
  $ ./vcd_dump --summary bad.vcd
  file:      bad.vcd
  date:      (none)
  version:   (none)
  timescale: (none)
  scopes:    1  vars: 1
  parse error: unexpected '%' at bad.vcd line 7 col 0
  [1]
